WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 297: Henry II: The Angevin Empire, the Common Law, and the Murder of Thomas Becket

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:23.1000
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series explores the brilliant, tempestuous founder of the Plantagenet dynasty, whose legal reforms established the English common law, but whose bitter feud with Archbishop Thomas Becket led to one of the most infamous assassinations of the Middle Ages.

4
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.520
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of King Henry I.

5
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:39.360
[George] Born in March 1133 in Le Mans, Henry was the eldest son of Empress Matilda and Geoffrey Plantagenet, Count of Anjou.

6
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:50.240
[George] Compact, stocky, with fiery red hair, freckled skin, and grey eyes that flashed with terrifying intensity, Henry possessed a relentless, manic physical energy.

7
00:00:50.240 --> 00:01:00.800
[George] He never sat down except when eating or riding, rode horses until they dropped dead beneath him, and dictated letters to three scribes simultaneously while holding court.

8
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.200
[George] In May 1152, 19-year-old Henry pulled off the diplomatic coup of the 12th century.

9
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:15.600
[George] He married the 30-year-old Duchess Alina of Aquitaine, barely eight weeks after her marriage to King Louis Vii of France had been annulled.

10
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:22.560
[George] Elina brought to Henry the vast, sun-drenched Duchy of Aquitaine, stretching from the Loire to the Pyrenees.

11
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:36.080
[George] Crowned King of England at Westminster in December 1154 at age 21, Henry was the absolute master of the Angevin Empire, ruling England, Normandy, Anjou, Maine, Turin, and Aquitaine.

12
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:55.040
[George] Henry Plantagenet was a physical and intellectual hurricane, stocky, bull-necked, and blessed with superhuman stamina, he rode horses until they collapsed beneath him, woke before dawn to hunt stag through dense forests, and wore simple leather hunting clothes that appalled the silk-robed courtiers of France.

13
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] His marriage to Alina of Aquitaine was an explosive union of two colossal personalities.

14
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:09.320
[George] Alina was ten years older, cultured, beautiful and fiercely independent, bringing to the English crown the vast Duchy of Aquitaine.

15
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:13.880
[George] Stretching from the wine valleys of Bordeaux to the rocky peaks of the Pyrenees.

16
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:22.920
[George] Together, they ruled an empire that was the commercial and military envy of Christendom, controlling every Atlantic port from the Solway Firth to Spain.

17
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:31.720
[George] Inheriting an England scarred by nineteen years of civil war, Henry moved with ruthless, surgical efficiency to restore royal authority.

18
00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:48.200
[George] He ordered the immediate demolition of over three hundred illegal adulterant castles built by robber barons during Stephen's reign, expelled Flemish mercenary bands from the realm, recalled alienated royal forest lands, and restored the centralized treasury and exchequer.

19
00:02:48.200 --> 00:03:04.680
[George] Henry travelled constantly on royal progresses, descending upon surprised provincial sheriffs to review royal finances, sleeping in rural hunting lodges, and demonstrating an extraordinary capacity for administrative work that left his weary courtiers begging for rest.

20
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:14.840
[George] Arriving in England in December 1154, 21-year-old Henry moved with lightning speed to dismantle the fortified anarchy left behind by King Stephen.

21
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:21.480
[George] He issued royal orders commanding the immediate demolition of every adulteran castle built without royal license.

22
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:26.760
[George] Personnelment leading siege engines to crush stubborn barons who refused to yield.

23
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:39.880
[George] Within six years, over three hundred illicit fortresses were flattened into rubble, the royal forests and domain lands were recovered for the crown, and royal revenue through the exchequer was restored to the levels of Henry I.

24
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.920
[George] Henry was a master of surprise inspections.

25
00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:50.320
[George] He would arrive unannounced at provincial sheriff courts at midnight, auditing ledgers and punishing corrupt bailiffs on the spot.

26
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.960
[George] Henry I.

27
00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:16.560
[George] Prior to Henry, justice was fragmented across hundreds of corrupt manorial and baronial courts that resolved disputes through superstitious trial by ordeal, forcing accused men to carry red-hot iron bars or plunge their arms into boiling water, declaring them guilty if their wounds festered after three days.

28
00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.560
[George] In the landmark assize of Clarendon in 1166, Henry revolutionized jurisprudence.

29
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:29.600
[George] He created standardized royal writs that ordinary freemen could purchase to seek justice directly from crown courts.

30
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:48.800
[George] Established permanent circuit courts of traveling royal justices, and introduced the revolutionary concept of the Grand Assize, a panel of twelve free and lawful local men sworn to declare the truth under oath, inventing the modern jury trial and establishing a uniform body of law common to all England.

31
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.520
[George] The legal revolution orchestrated by Henry I.

32
00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:59.120
[George] at the assize of Clarendon in 1166 laid the foundational bedrock of all English and American jurisprudence.

33
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:13.200
[George] By creating standardized royal writs, such as the novel De Sason and Mort Dancester, Henry gave ordinary freemen affordable access to the king's courts, stripping feudal barons of their arbitrary powers to dispossess tenants.

34
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:29.960
[George] Henry's introduction of the Grand Assize abolished the primitive superstition of trial by ordeal, where accused men were burned with hot irons or plunged into cold water to determine guilt, replacing it with sworn panels of twelve local freeholders who weighed factual evidence.

35
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:36.440
[George] Inventing the modern jury system and creating a unified body of law, common to the entire realm.

36
00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:48.360
[George] In his early reign, Henry's closest friend, confidant, and partner in pleasure was Thomas Becket, a brilliant, charismatic London merchant's son whom Henry appointed Lord Chancellor.

37
00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:58.440
[George] Becket lived with extravagant luxury, riding through Europe with sixty carriage horses and banqueting in silk robes, sharing an intimate bond with the young king.

38
00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:13.160
[George] In 1162, seeking to bring the independent church courts under royal jurisdiction, Henry made the fatal calculation of elevating Becket to Archbishop of Canterbury, believing his drinking companion would remain his pliant royal servant.

39
00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.960
[George] To Henry's fury, Becket underwent an overnight spiritual conversion.

40
00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:29.080
[George] He resigned the chancellorship, adopted coarse hairshirts infested with lice, washed the feet of thirteen lepers daily, and became an uncompromising champion of papal supremacy.

41
00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:42.440
[George] When Henry issued the constitutions of Clarendon in 1164, requiring clergy accused of felonies to be tried in royal courts, Becket refused to submit, fleeing into six years of bitter French exile.

42
00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.600
[George] The tragic clash between Henry I.

43
00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:59.000
[George] During their youth, the king and his brilliant Lord Chancellor were inseparable companions who shared hunting lodges, royal secrets, and diplomatic triumphs.

44
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:14.440
[George] When Henry elevated Becket to Archbishop of Canterbury in 1162, intending to bring criminal clerics under royal judicial control, Beckett's sudden, radical spiritual transformation into a champion of clerical immunity infuriated the king.

45
00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:34.880
[George] Henry's constitutions of Clarendon in 1164 sought to end the scandal of criminal clerks, where murderers and thieves avoided the gallows by claiming clerical status and being tried in lenient church courts, sparking a bitter six-year war of words that drove Becket into French exile and tore the English church apart.

46
00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:42.320
[George] In 1170, a fragile, hollow reconciliation was negotiated, allowing Becket to return to Canterbury.

47
00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:52.640
[George] But the moment Becket arrived on English shores, he immediately excommunicated the Archbishop of York and two other bishops who had dared to crown Henry's eldest son in his absence.

48
00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.960
[George] At Christmas 1170, holding court at Burrs in Normandy, the news reached King Henry.

49
00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:10.240
[George] Overcome by a blinding volcanic rage, Henry hurled himself to the floor, gnawing on straw from his mattress and screaming the fatal, exasperated words that echoed through history.

50
00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:19.040
[George] What miserable drones and traitors have I nourished in my household, that they let their lord be treated with such shameful contempt by a low-born cleric.

51
00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:22.000
[George] Will no one rid me of this turbulent priest?

52
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:34.960
[George] Four knights, Reginald Fitz Erse, William de Tracy, Hugh de Morville, and Richard Le Breton, took the king at his literal word, slipping out of the hall, galloping to the coast, and boarding ships for England.

53
00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:44.240
[George] The fatal outburst at Burrs in Normandy at Christmas 1170 was the catastrophic culmination of years of suppressed rage and political frustration.

54
00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:51.600
[George] When news arrived that Beckett had excommunicated the bishops who had crowned Henry's heir, the king suffered one of his legendary.

55
00:08:51.600 --> 00:09:00.680
[George] Volcanic Angevin temper tantrums, rolling on the rushes and shouting the exasperated words that his retainers interpreted as a royal execution order.

56
00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:19.240
[George] The four knights who rode to Canterbury, Fitz Erse, Tracy, Morville and Le Breton, viewed themselves not as assassins, but as loyal vassals executing the unwritten will of their sovereign, crossing the channel in stormy seas and riding hard to the cathedral precincts with murder in their hearts.

57
00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:29.080
[George] On the dark winter afternoon of December 29, 1170, the four armed knights burst into Canterbury Cathedral as the monks were singing vespers.

58
00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:36.360
[George] Demanding that Becket absolve the excommunicated bishops, the knights drew broadswords when Becket defiantly refused.

59
00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.800
[George] I am ready to die for my lord, that in my blood the Church may obtain liberty and peace.

60
00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:44.520
[George] Surrounding the Archbishop near the altar of St.

61
00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:47.080
[George] Benedict, the knights struck.

62
00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:57.480
[George] Tracy's sword severed Becket's skull, and a renegade subdeacon stepped forward, using the point of his sword to scatter the martyrs' brains and blood across the stone pavement.

63
00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:59.960
[George] The assassination shocked Christendom.

64
00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:03.560
[George] Pope Alexander III excommunicated the murderers.

65
00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:10.440
[George] Becket was canonized as a saint in record time in 1173, and miracles were reported at his tomb.

66
00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:13.160
[George] Horrified by the monster he had unleashed.

67
00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:42.680
[George] The butchery of Archbishop Thomas Becket inside his own cathedral on December 29, 1170, was an atrocity that sent shockwaves of horror across Western Christendom, as Becket clung to a stone pillar near the altar of St.

68
00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:51.280
[George] Benedict, Tracy's broadsword severed the top of his skull, and the knights scattered his brains across the pavement before fleeing into the night.

69
00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:57.680
[George] Becket was immediately crowned an immortal martyr of the church, canonized by Pope Alexander I.i.i.

70
00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:02.880
[George] within two years, while Canterbury became the premier pilgrimage destination of Europe.

71
00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:10.080
[George] Henry's public penance at Canterbury in July 1174 was an extraordinary act of sovereign humiliation.

72
00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:16.320
[George] Stripped to a rough wool shirt, the King of England walked barefoot on sharp stones to Becket's tomb.

73
00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:21.280
[George] Weeping as eighty monks scoured his back with rods until he collapsed in exhaustion.

74
00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:25.920
[George] Henry's final decade was an agonizing tragedy of familial betrayal.

75
00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:41.920
[George] His proud wife Alina of Aquitaine, resentful of his open adulterous affairs with fair Rosamund Clifford, incited their ambitious sons, young Henry, Richard the Lionheart, Geoffrey, and John into repeated armed rebellions against their father.

76
00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:48.560
[George] Henry imprisoned a leaner in Winchester Castle for sixteen years, but his son's betrayal never ceased.

77
00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:57.600
[George] In July 1189, sick with a bleeding ulcer and dying at Chinon Castle, Henry was forced to surrender to his son Richard and King Philip I.

78
00:11:57.920 --> 00:11:58.800
[George] of France.

79
00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:06.400
[George] Asking for the list of traitors who had allied with Richard, Henry saw the very first name, his beloved youngest son John.

80
00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:14.320
[George] Turning his face to the wall in broken hearted despair, the dying sovereign whispered, Shame, shame on a conquered king.

81
00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:19.960
[George] Passing away on July 6, 1189, at age 56, King Henry I.I.

82
00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.320
[George] was buried at Font of Rod Abbey.

83
00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:25.640
[George] Despite his tragic domestic ruin, Henry I.I.

84
00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:29.160
[George] remains the greatest institutional monarch of the medieval era.

85
00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:36.280
[George] The legal architect whose common law and jury system established the bedrock of Anglo American liberty for all time.

86
00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:50.200
[George] Henry's final years were consumed by the tragic fratricidal rebellion of his four sons, young Henry, Richard the Lionheart, Geoffrey of Brittany, and John, who were backed by their imprisoned mother Alina and King Philip I.

87
00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:54.680
[George] of France in an unholy alliance to dismember the Angevin Empire.

88
00:12:54.680 --> 00:13:05.080
[George] Trapped and dying of a perforated ulcer at Chinnon Castle in July 1189, Henry was forced to submit to humiliating peace terms dictated by his son Richard.

89
00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:13.160
[George] When the dying monarch was shown the list of traitors and read the name of his favorite son, Prince John, his broken heart surrendered.

90
00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:15.080
[George] Let things go as they will.

91
00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:18.200
[George] I care no more for myself or for this world.

92
00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:22.440
[George] Passing away on July 6, 1189, King Henry I.

93
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:31.880
[George] Leaving behind an immortal legacy as the royal lion whose legal genius founded the English common law.

94
00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:51.080
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with his famous warrior son, Richard the Lionheart and the glory of the Third Crusade.

95
00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:53.560
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

